{% extends 'base.en.html.twig' %}
{% set nav_actif = 'notation' %}
{% set route_fr = 'paleo_notation' %}
{% set route_de = 'paleo_de_notation' %}

{% block titre %}The basics of the notation{% endblock %}
{% block meta_description %}Staff, clefs and the basic neumes of Gregorian chant (punctum, pes, clivis, torculus…) explained with simple diagrams.{% endblock %}

{% block contenu %}
<h1>The basics&nbsp;: learning to see a neume</h1>
<p class="chapeau">
    Before opening the <em>Graduale Triplex</em>, let us set up a few simple landmarks.
    By the end of this page you will recognise the Gregorian staff, its clef, and about a
    dozen fundamental neumes.
</p>

<h2>1. The four-line staff</h2>
<p class="lettrine">
    Our modern staff has five lines. Gregorian chant, by contrast, is written on a staff of
    only <strong>four lines</strong> (the <span class="mot-latin">tetragram</span>). That is
    enough, because Gregorian melodies move within a fairly narrow range. As today, the
    <em>higher</em> a sign sits on the staff, the <em>higher</em> the sound&nbsp;; the
    <em>lower</em> it sits, the <em>lower</em> the sound. One peculiarity worth knowing&nbsp;:
    <strong>the clef can sit on different lines</strong> — it is not fixed once and for all as
    in modern music, and it sometimes even changes in the middle of a piece.
</p>

<div class="encadre def">
    <span class="etiquette">Definition — The clef</span>
    <p>
        A <strong>clef</strong> is a marker placed at the start of a line that names one of
        the lines. In chant, two clefs are mainly used&nbsp;: the
        <strong><em>C</em> clef</strong> (do) <img src="{{ asset('assets/img/cle-ut.png') }}" alt="C clef" class="icone-cle">
        and the <strong><em>F</em> clef</strong> <img src="{{ asset('assets/img/cle-fa.png') }}" alt="F clef" class="icone-cle">.
        They do not fix an absolute pitch
        (the choir adjusts the pitch to its voices), but they show where the semitones fall,
        which is enough to sing in tune.
    </p>
</div>

<h2>2. The basic note&nbsp;: the punctum</h2>
<p>
    In square notation the commonest single note is the
    <strong><span class="mot-latin">punctum</span></strong> — literally a «&nbsp;dot&nbsp;»,
    drawn as a small square. A variant, the <strong><span class="mot-latin">virga</span></strong>
    («&nbsp;rod&nbsp;»), is a square extended by a small stem&nbsp;; it often notes a higher
    note. When reading, <em>punctum</em> and <em>virga</em> are both worth a single note.
</p>

<div class="galerie-neumes">
    <div class="carte-neume">
        <img src="{{ asset('assets/img/neume-punctum.jpg') }}" alt="A punctum: a single square note, here on '-tus' of 'natus', Graduale Triplex p. 47">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 160 90" role="img" aria-label="Modern transcription: one note on 'tus'" class="svg-portee-moderne">
            <line x1="34" y1="18" x2="150" y2="18" class="svg-portee"/>
            <line x1="34" y1="28" x2="150" y2="28" class="svg-portee"/>
            <line x1="34" y1="38" x2="150" y2="38" class="svg-portee"/>
            <line x1="34" y1="48" x2="150" y2="48" class="svg-portee"/>
            <line x1="34" y1="58" x2="150" y2="58" class="svg-portee"/>
            <image href="{{ asset('assets/img/treble-clef.png') }}" x="2" y="8" width="36" height="58"/>
            <ellipse cx="90" cy="38" rx="5" ry="3.5" class="svg-neume"/>
            <text x="90" y="82" font-family="serif" font-size="13" text-anchor="middle" class="svg-neume">tus</text>
        </svg>
        <span class="nom">Punctum</span>
        <span class="desc">1 note — "na-<strong>tus</strong>", p.&nbsp;47</span>
    </div>
    <div class="carte-neume">
        <img src="{{ asset('assets/img/neume-virga.jpg') }}" alt="A virga: a square note with a stem, here on 'impe-', Graduale Triplex p. 47">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 160 90" role="img" aria-label="Modern transcription: one note on 'pe'" class="svg-portee-moderne">
            <line x1="34" y1="18" x2="150" y2="18" class="svg-portee"/>
            <line x1="34" y1="28" x2="150" y2="28" class="svg-portee"/>
            <line x1="34" y1="38" x2="150" y2="38" class="svg-portee"/>
            <line x1="34" y1="48" x2="150" y2="48" class="svg-portee"/>
            <line x1="34" y1="58" x2="150" y2="58" class="svg-portee"/>
            <image href="{{ asset('assets/img/treble-clef.png') }}" x="2" y="8" width="36" height="58"/>
            <ellipse cx="90" cy="28" rx="5" ry="3.5" class="svg-neume"/>
            <text x="90" y="82" font-family="serif" font-size="13" text-anchor="middle" class="svg-neume">pe-</text>
        </svg>
        <span class="nom">Virga</span>
        <span class="desc">1 note (often high) — "<strong>impe</strong>-rium", p.&nbsp;47</span>
    </div>
</div>

<h2>3. Two-note neumes</h2>
<p>
    Things get more complex — and more interesting — when several sounds are sung
    <strong>on a single syllable</strong>. They are then grouped into a single drawing.
    The golden rule for reading&nbsp;: <strong>a neume is read from bottom to top and from
    left to right</strong>, following the stroke.
</p>

<div class="galerie-neumes">
    <div class="carte-neume">
        <img src="{{ asset('assets/img/neume-pes.jpg') }}" alt="A pes: two notes joined vertically, ascending, here on 'da-', Graduale Triplex p. 47">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 160 90" role="img" aria-label="Modern transcription: two ascending notes on 'da'" class="svg-portee-moderne">
            <line x1="34" y1="18" x2="150" y2="18" class="svg-portee"/>
            <line x1="34" y1="28" x2="150" y2="28" class="svg-portee"/>
            <line x1="34" y1="38" x2="150" y2="38" class="svg-portee"/>
            <line x1="34" y1="48" x2="150" y2="48" class="svg-portee"/>
            <line x1="34" y1="58" x2="150" y2="58" class="svg-portee"/>
            <image href="{{ asset('assets/img/treble-clef.png') }}" x="2" y="8" width="36" height="58"/>
            <path d="M75,39 Q90,20 105,19" class="svg-slur"/>
            <ellipse cx="80" cy="48" rx="5" ry="3.5" class="svg-neume"/>
            <ellipse cx="100" cy="28" rx="5" ry="3.5" class="svg-neume"/>
            <text x="90" y="82" font-family="serif" font-size="13" text-anchor="middle" class="svg-neume">da-</text>
        </svg>
        <span class="nom">Pes <span class="mot-latin">(podatus)</span></span>
        <span class="desc">2 notes going up — "<strong>da</strong>-tus", p.&nbsp;47</span>
    </div>
    <div class="carte-neume">
        <img src="{{ asset('assets/img/neume-clivis.jpg') }}" alt="A clivis: two joined notes, descending, here on 'vá-', Graduale Triplex p. 47">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 160 90" role="img" aria-label="Modern transcription: two descending notes on 'vá'" class="svg-portee-moderne">
            <line x1="34" y1="18" x2="150" y2="18" class="svg-portee"/>
            <line x1="34" y1="28" x2="150" y2="28" class="svg-portee"/>
            <line x1="34" y1="38" x2="150" y2="38" class="svg-portee"/>
            <line x1="34" y1="48" x2="150" y2="48" class="svg-portee"/>
            <line x1="34" y1="58" x2="150" y2="58" class="svg-portee"/>
            <image href="{{ asset('assets/img/treble-clef.png') }}" x="2" y="8" width="36" height="58"/>
            <path d="M75,20 Q90,17 105,40" class="svg-slur"/>
            <ellipse cx="80" cy="28" rx="5" ry="3.5" class="svg-neume"/>
            <ellipse cx="100" cy="48" rx="5" ry="3.5" class="svg-neume"/>
            <text x="90" y="82" font-family="serif" font-size="13" text-anchor="middle" class="svg-neume">vá-</text>
        </svg>
        <span class="nom">Clivis <span class="mot-latin">(flexa)</span></span>
        <span class="desc">2 notes going down — "<strong>vá</strong>-tor", p.&nbsp;47</span>
    </div>
</div>

<h2>4. Three-note neumes</h2>
<p>
    With three notes you draw small «&nbsp;waves&nbsp;». Remember these four above all —
    you will meet them constantly&nbsp;:
</p>

<div class="galerie-neumes">
    <div class="carte-neume">
        <img src="{{ asset('assets/img/neume-torculus.jpg') }}" alt="A torculus: low, high, low, here on 'na-' of 'natus', Graduale Triplex p. 47">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 160 90" role="img" aria-label="Modern transcription: low-high-low on 'na'" class="svg-portee-moderne">
            <line x1="34" y1="18" x2="150" y2="18" class="svg-portee"/>
            <line x1="34" y1="28" x2="150" y2="28" class="svg-portee"/>
            <line x1="34" y1="38" x2="150" y2="38" class="svg-portee"/>
            <line x1="34" y1="48" x2="150" y2="48" class="svg-portee"/>
            <line x1="34" y1="58" x2="150" y2="58" class="svg-portee"/>
            <image href="{{ asset('assets/img/treble-clef.png') }}" x="2" y="8" width="36" height="58"/>
            <path d="M65,40 Q90,17 115,40" class="svg-slur"/>
            <ellipse cx="70" cy="48" rx="5" ry="3.5" class="svg-neume"/>
            <ellipse cx="90" cy="28" rx="5" ry="3.5" class="svg-neume"/>
            <ellipse cx="110" cy="48" rx="5" ry="3.5" class="svg-neume"/>
            <text x="90" y="82" font-family="serif" font-size="13" text-anchor="middle" class="svg-neume">na-</text>
        </svg>
        <span class="nom">Torculus</span>
        <span class="desc">low · high · low — "<strong>na</strong>-tus", p.&nbsp;47</span>
    </div>
    <div class="carte-neume">
        <img src="{{ asset('assets/img/neume-porrectus.jpg') }}" alt="A porrectus: high, low, high, drawn as a single curved stroke, Graduale Triplex p. 45">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 160 90" role="img" aria-label="Modern transcription: high-low-high" class="svg-portee-moderne">
            <line x1="34" y1="18" x2="150" y2="18" class="svg-portee"/>
            <line x1="34" y1="28" x2="150" y2="28" class="svg-portee"/>
            <line x1="34" y1="38" x2="150" y2="38" class="svg-portee"/>
            <line x1="34" y1="48" x2="150" y2="48" class="svg-portee"/>
            <line x1="34" y1="58" x2="150" y2="58" class="svg-portee"/>
            <image href="{{ asset('assets/img/treble-clef.png') }}" x="2" y="8" width="36" height="58"/>
            <path d="M65,20 Q90,10 115,30" class="svg-slur"/>
            <ellipse cx="70" cy="28" rx="5" ry="3.5" class="svg-neume"/>
            <ellipse cx="90" cy="58" rx="5" ry="3.5" class="svg-neume"/>
            <ellipse cx="110" cy="38" rx="5" ry="3.5" class="svg-neume"/>
        </svg>
        <span class="nom">Porrectus</span>
        <span class="desc">high · low · high, one curved stroke — p.&nbsp;45</span>
    </div>
    <div class="carte-neume">
        <img src="{{ asset('assets/img/neume-scandicus.jpg') }}" alt="A scandicus: notes climbing in a staircase, here on 'De-', Graduale Triplex p. 48">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 160 90" role="img" aria-label="Modern transcription: ascending notes on 'De'" class="svg-portee-moderne">
            <line x1="34" y1="18" x2="150" y2="18" class="svg-portee"/>
            <line x1="34" y1="28" x2="150" y2="28" class="svg-portee"/>
            <line x1="34" y1="38" x2="150" y2="38" class="svg-portee"/>
            <line x1="34" y1="48" x2="150" y2="48" class="svg-portee"/>
            <line x1="34" y1="58" x2="150" y2="58" class="svg-portee"/>
            <image href="{{ asset('assets/img/treble-clef.png') }}" x="2" y="8" width="36" height="58"/>
            <path d="M60,50 Q95,12 130,20" class="svg-slur"/>
            <ellipse cx="65" cy="58" rx="5" ry="3.5" class="svg-neume"/>
            <ellipse cx="85" cy="48" rx="5" ry="3.5" class="svg-neume"/>
            <ellipse cx="105" cy="38" rx="5" ry="3.5" class="svg-neume"/>
            <ellipse cx="125" cy="28" rx="5" ry="3.5" class="svg-neume"/>
            <text x="95" y="82" font-family="serif" font-size="13" text-anchor="middle" class="svg-neume">De-</text>
        </svg>
        <span class="nom">Scandicus</span>
        <span class="desc">notes going up — "<strong>De</strong>-i", p.&nbsp;48</span>
    </div>
    <div class="carte-neume">
        <img src="{{ asset('assets/img/neume-climacus.jpg') }}" alt="A climacus: a high note then descending notes, here on 'bis', Graduale Triplex p. 47">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 160 90" role="img" aria-label="Modern transcription: descending notes on 'bis'" class="svg-portee-moderne">
            <line x1="34" y1="18" x2="150" y2="18" class="svg-portee"/>
            <line x1="34" y1="28" x2="150" y2="28" class="svg-portee"/>
            <line x1="34" y1="38" x2="150" y2="38" class="svg-portee"/>
            <line x1="34" y1="48" x2="150" y2="48" class="svg-portee"/>
            <line x1="34" y1="58" x2="150" y2="58" class="svg-portee"/>
            <image href="{{ asset('assets/img/treble-clef.png') }}" x="2" y="8" width="36" height="58"/>
            <path d="M70,20 Q95,17 120,40" class="svg-slur"/>
            <ellipse cx="75" cy="28" rx="5" ry="3.5" class="svg-neume"/>
            <ellipse cx="95" cy="38" rx="5" ry="3.5" class="svg-neume"/>
            <ellipse cx="115" cy="48" rx="5" ry="3.5" class="svg-neume"/>
            <text x="95" y="82" font-family="serif" font-size="13" text-anchor="middle" class="svg-neume">bis</text>
        </svg>
        <span class="nom">Climacus</span>
        <span class="desc">1 note then a descent — "<strong>bis</strong>", p.&nbsp;47</span>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="encadre astuce">
    <span class="etiquette">Memory tip</span>
    <p>
        Many neume names «&nbsp;say&nbsp;» their own shape&nbsp;:
        <span class="mot-latin">scandicus</span> suggests climbing (as in «&nbsp;escalade&nbsp;»),
        <span class="mot-latin">climacus</span> going down a staircase (Greek <em>klimax</em>
        = ladder), <span class="mot-latin">flexa</span> the bend downwards. Trust the
        <strong>shape</strong> before memorising the name.
    </p>
</div>

<h2>5. Two signs that change the colour of the chant</h2>
<p>
    Some small signs do not change the note, but the <em>way</em> it is sung.
    Here are two essential ones, which we shall meet again in the examples&nbsp;:
</p>
<ul>
    <li>
        <strong>The <span class="mot-latin">quilisma</span></strong> — a note drawn as a
        zigzag, light and fleeting, which acts as a springboard to the next note&nbsp;;
        traditionally the preceding note is slightly lengthened.
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong>The episema</strong> — a small horizontal stroke added above (or beside) a
        neume to indicate that the note is <em>settled on</em>, <em>broadened</em>. It is the
        equivalent of a slight «&nbsp;leaning&nbsp;» of the voice.
    </li>
</ul>

<div class="galerie-neumes">
    <div class="carte-neume">
        <svg width="140" height="76" viewBox="0 0 140 76" role="img" aria-label="A quilisma: a note drawn as a zigzag">
            <line x1="10" y1="20" x2="130" y2="20" class="svg-portee"/>
            <line x1="10" y1="32" x2="130" y2="32" class="svg-portee"/>
            <line x1="10" y1="44" x2="130" y2="44" class="svg-portee"/>
            <line x1="10" y1="56" x2="130" y2="56" class="svg-portee"/>
            <rect x="52" y="46" width="12" height="10" rx="1" class="svg-neume"/>
            <path d="M66 44 l4 -6 l3 6 l4 -6 l3 6" fill="none" class="svg-neume" stroke-width="2"/>
            <rect x="82" y="20" width="12" height="10" rx="1" class="svg-neume"/>
            <rect x="92" y="24" width="2" height="14" class="svg-neume"/>
        </svg>
        <svg viewBox="0 0 160 90" role="img" aria-label="Modern transcription: ascending notes with quilisma ornament" class="svg-portee-moderne">
            <line x1="34" y1="18" x2="150" y2="18" class="svg-portee"/>
            <line x1="34" y1="28" x2="150" y2="28" class="svg-portee"/>
            <line x1="34" y1="38" x2="150" y2="38" class="svg-portee"/>
            <line x1="34" y1="48" x2="150" y2="48" class="svg-portee"/>
            <line x1="34" y1="58" x2="150" y2="58" class="svg-portee"/>
            <image href="{{ asset('assets/img/treble-clef.png') }}" x="2" y="8" width="36" height="58"/>
            <path d="M65,50 Q95,10 125,20" class="svg-slur"/>
            <ellipse cx="70" cy="58" rx="5" ry="3.5" class="svg-neume"/>
            <ellipse cx="95" cy="43" rx="5" ry="3.5" class="svg-neume"/>
            <path d="M89,33 l3,-5 l2.5,5 l3,-5 l2.5,5" class="svg-orn" stroke-width="1.5"/>
            <ellipse cx="120" cy="28" rx="5" ry="3.5" class="svg-neume"/>
        </svg>
        <span class="nom">Quilisma</span>
        <span class="desc">light note, springboard</span>
    </div>
    <div class="carte-neume">
        <svg width="140" height="76" viewBox="0 0 140 76" role="img" aria-label="A note with an episema: a horizontal stroke marks a leaning">
            <line x1="10" y1="20" x2="130" y2="20" class="svg-portee"/>
            <line x1="10" y1="32" x2="130" y2="32" class="svg-portee"/>
            <line x1="10" y1="44" x2="130" y2="44" class="svg-portee"/>
            <line x1="10" y1="56" x2="130" y2="56" class="svg-portee"/>
            <rect x="64" y="34" width="12" height="10" rx="1" class="svg-neume"/>
            <rect x="62" y="30" width="16" height="2" class="svg-neume"/>
        </svg>
        <span class="nom">Episema</span>
        <span class="desc">settle / broaden</span>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="encadre">
    <span class="etiquette">Key point</span>
    <p>
        A neume = a drawing read from left to right and from bottom to top. Its
        <strong>shape</strong> tells the movement of the voice&nbsp;; a few added signs
        (episema, quilisma) tell the <strong>manner</strong>. You now have all the basic
        vocabulary needed to tackle the book that brings them together.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="suite">
